Myth: On the twin peaks of Aethelgard, two keepers watched the world from their isolated summits. Between them lay a deep, impassable gorge. One held a vessel of raw embers, the other, a vessel of crystalline frost. The Weaver of the Peaks knew that to hold the embers was to watch them fade into gray ash, while to cast them across the chasm was to risk losing their warmth to the abyss. Yet, he chose to fling the embers into the air. Mid-flight, the mountain winds whipped the sparks, tripling their heat until they landed as radiant, pulsing diamonds in the frost-keeper’s hands. The second keeper, witness to this leap of faith, recognized the bond formed by the fire. He could have absorbed the warmth, leaving his partner in the cold, but he knew the law of the peaks: fire must be shared to survive. He returned half the glowing remnants back across the void, bridging the distance with golden light. From that day, the keepers never again feared the dark. They learned that the spark only grows when released, and that every act of trust, when met with a cycle of return, keeps the mountain paths illuminated for both.
